course: dictionary
lesson: 2632
title: Despite and contrast
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Despite is a joining word that links two facts that seem not to fit together. It means even though something else is true, or without being stopped by something. «The inner core stays solid despite the heat» tells you the heat could have melted it, but it did not.
explain: Look for the surprise. Ask yourself: what did I expect to happen, and what actually happened? «Despite their name, no centipede has exactly one hundred legs.» The name suggests a hundred legs, but the fact is different.
explain: Despite works like although and but. «Although it was raining, we played outside» and «It was raining, but we played outside» mean the same as «Despite the rain, we played outside». Swap one in to check that the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Despite means without being stopped by something, or even though something else is true.
fact: «Despite the rain» means the rain did not stop the thing from happening.
fact: «Despite their name» is a common phrase: the name suggests one thing, but the truth is different.
fact: Despite is a preposition, so it is followed by a noun or an -ing word, like «despite being tired».
fact: «Despite the fact that» is followed by a whole sentence, like «despite the fact that it was late».
exercise: «The inner core stays solid despite the heat.» What does despite show? | The core moves faster in the heat | The core is hot, so it must melt | The heat makes the core solid | *The heat could have melted it, but it stays solid anyway
exercise: «Despite their name, no centipede has exactly one hundred legs.» What does this tell us? | Centipedes have a name for each leg | Centipedes are very quick | Centipedes have exactly one hundred legs | *The name suggests one hundred legs, but the truth is different
exercise: Which word means the same as despite in «Despite being tired, she finished the race»? | Until | Because | *Although | Before
test: «Despite the rain, the match went ahead.» What does despite mean here? | The match was faster because of the rain | The rain stopped before the match began | The rain cancelled the match | *Even though it was raining, the match still went ahead
test: «Tom was sad despite winning the match.» What does despite tell you? | He lost the match | *Winning should have made him happy, but he was still sad | He won, so he was happy | He was sad because he won
exam: «Despite the name, the moonrat is not a rat.» What does despite tell you? | *You might expect a rat, but the moonrat is something different | The moonrat is a rat, as its name says | The moonrat is called a rat because it is one | The moonrat has no name
